An existing file whose name has glob characters (`x[1].rs`), given with a trailing `/`, is reported as not a directory, as any file with a trailing `/` is: the failed lookup of the literal path decides, so the argument doesn't turn into a glob pattern that matches nothing. outln exits 1.
== args
input/x[1].rs/
== file input/x[1].rs
//! x
== stdout
== stderr
File input/x[1].rs/ is not a directory; remove the trailing /
== exit 1
